Driver and manufacturer cross-check (not additional connectivity tests)
  Kernel: 7.3.4-zen2-2-zen. System enumeration identifies 1a86:7523 as a
  CH340-family adapter (ch341/ch341-uart), and 359f:3101 as SIPEED UARTx4 HS
  (cdc_acm). The connected ACM interface reports UART-1 HS. The USB IDs do
  not determine the exact chip revision or the Sipeed firmware version.

  CH340: WCH's CH340DS1 specifies 50 bps to 2 Mbps for its UART. This is a
  datasheet rating, not a verified limit for this specific adapter. The Zen
  ch341 driver clamps requested nonzero rates to 46-3000000 when computing
  its divider. Attainable nominal rates depend on integer division and the
  prescaler; there is no fixed driver list of allowed rates. The adapter's
  'using simulated break' kernel log also identifies its limited-prescaler
  quirk. The successful set path does not encode the divider's resulting baud
  back into termios. A 10000000 readback thus does not establish a 10 Mbaud link.

  Sipeed: the product documentation for SLogic Combo 8 UARTx4 HS lists maxima
  of 20 Mbaud on the first two hardware UARTs and 1 Mbaud on the other two
  GPIO UARTs. It does not specify a minimum or full set of supported baud
  rates. Linux cdc_acm sends the requested rate in USB CDC SET_LINE_CODING;
  a termios readback does not verify what the device put on its UART pins.

  The 1221-baud result is for the linked pair, not an isolated IC rating.
  It does not prove that every untested rate below it is unusable or that
  rates above it all work. No upper limit or long transfer was tested here.

